1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,260
Eight people went to bed in one house in Villisca, Iowa.

2
00:00:05,310 --> 00:00:08,559
By morning, all eight were dead.

3
00:00:08,609 --> 00:00:10,429
No one was ever convicted.

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:13,109
But the strangest thing about this case

5
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:15,179
is not what went missing.

6
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:16,570
It's what kept showing up.

7
00:00:21,626 --> 00:00:25,636
This is the Villisca axe murder case.

8
00:00:25,686 --> 00:00:28,016
June, nineteen twelve.

9
00:00:28,066 --> 00:00:29,886
Joe and Sarah Moore, their four children,

10
00:00:29,936 --> 00:00:31,396
and two young girls who stayed the night.

11
00:00:31,446 --> 00:00:34,606
I'm not going to tell you who did it.

12
00:00:34,656 --> 00:00:39,166
I'm going to show you the record.

13
00:00:39,216 --> 00:00:42,606
Court testimony, and the newspapers of the time.

14
00:00:42,656 --> 00:00:45,176
What was found in that house.

15
00:00:45,226 --> 00:00:49,396
And everything that was added over the next nineteen years.

16
00:00:49,446 --> 00:00:51,856
Welcome to story timez.

17
00:00:51,906 --> 00:00:54,766
If you like your true crime told from the record,

18
00:00:54,816 --> 00:00:55,666
subscribe,

19
00:00:55,716 --> 00:00:58,346
and tell me in the comments which case I should open next.

20
00:00:58,866 --> 00:01:01,136
Everyone who tells this story

21
00:01:01,186 --> 00:01:04,456
will tell you why it was never solved.

22
00:01:04,506 --> 00:01:06,926
A crowd walked through the rooms.

23
00:01:06,976 --> 00:01:09,206
The evidence was lost.

24
00:01:09,256 --> 00:01:11,836
And that is partly true.

25
00:01:11,886 --> 00:01:15,046
But here is what the paperwork shows.

26
00:01:15,096 --> 00:01:18,146
This case did not run out of evidence.

27
00:01:18,196 --> 00:01:21,886
For nineteen years

28
00:01:21,936 --> 00:01:23,366
it kept getting more.

29
00:01:23,416 --> 00:01:27,976
A closet.

30
00:01:28,026 --> 00:01:30,746
A photograph, said to come from inside a child's eye.

31
00:01:30,796 --> 00:01:32,556
A sound that turned into a man.

32
00:01:32,606 --> 00:01:33,206
A signed confession.

33
00:01:33,036 --> 00:01:33,826
A fingerprint.

34
00:01:34,348 --> 00:01:38,288
On the morning of June tenth, nineteen twelve,

35
00:01:38,338 --> 00:01:40,608
a mother in southwest Iowa

36
00:01:40,658 --> 00:01:43,248
telephoned a neighbor's house.

37
00:01:43,298 --> 00:01:46,258
Her husband told the coroner about it.

38
00:01:46,308 --> 00:01:48,398
My wife did, yes.

39
00:01:48,448 --> 00:01:50,548
He could not say what time.

40
00:01:50,598 --> 00:01:52,138
She tried, he said,

41
00:01:52,188 --> 00:01:54,918
about three different times.

42
00:01:54,968 --> 00:01:57,538
Her two daughters had stayed the night there.

43
00:01:57,588 --> 00:01:59,768
She expected them back.

44
00:01:59,818 --> 00:02:01,888
That house was locked.

45
00:02:01,938 --> 00:02:04,348
The blinds were down.

46
00:02:04,398 --> 00:02:08,148
I can't tell you who killed eight people in Villisca.

47
00:02:08,198 --> 00:02:09,678
No one can.

48
00:02:09,728 --> 00:02:12,888
What I can give you is an inventory.

49
00:02:12,938 --> 00:02:14,858
What was in that house.

50
00:02:14,908 --> 00:02:16,778
And what was carried in afterward.

51
00:02:17,365 --> 00:02:20,145
The first entry is the people.

52
00:02:20,194 --> 00:02:22,585
Joe Moore was forty-three.

53
00:02:22,634 --> 00:02:24,735
He sold farm implements.

54
00:02:24,784 --> 00:02:26,824
Sarah Moore was thirty-nine,

55
00:02:26,875 --> 00:02:29,844
and she helped run the Children's Day program

56
00:02:29,895 --> 00:02:31,735
at the Presbyterian church.

57
00:02:31,784 --> 00:02:33,544
Herman was eleven.

58
00:02:33,594 --> 00:02:34,769
That Sunday night,

59
00:02:34,819 --> 00:02:35,694
he spoke a piece

60
00:02:35,745 --> 00:02:38,375
about God watching over the sparrows.

61
00:02:38,425 --> 00:02:40,254
Katherine was ten.

62
00:02:40,305 --> 00:02:42,215
Boyd was seven.

63
00:02:42,264 --> 00:02:44,275
Paul was five.

64
00:02:44,324 --> 00:02:47,704
Lena Stillinger took part in the program too.

65
00:02:47,754 --> 00:02:49,955
Her sister Ina was with her.

66
00:02:50,004 --> 00:02:52,775
The records disagree about their ages.

67
00:02:52,824 --> 00:02:55,224
Lena was eleven or twelve.

68
00:02:55,275 --> 00:02:55,875
Ina was eight or nine,

69
00:02:55,724 --> 00:02:57,175
and one trial report says six.

70
00:02:57,224 --> 00:02:59,805
At six o'clock that evening,

71
00:02:59,855 --> 00:03:01,574
according to their older sister Blanche,

72
00:03:01,625 --> 00:03:04,165
Joe Moore telephoned the Stillinger home.

73
00:03:04,215 --> 00:03:07,215
He did not want the girls walking back in the dark.

74
00:03:07,264 --> 00:03:12,534
Could they stay with his family?

75
00:03:12,585 --> 00:03:15,215
Blanche said she thought that would be all right.

76
00:03:15,264 --> 00:03:19,775
The pastor testified the program ended about nine fifteen.

77
00:03:19,824 --> 00:03:21,718
Nobody who saw what came next

78
00:03:21,768 --> 00:03:23,795
ever gave an account that held.

79
00:03:23,844 --> 00:03:25,324
So I won't give you one.

80
00:03:25,779 --> 00:03:26,999
Monday morning.

81
00:03:27,049 --> 00:03:29,039
A neighbor, Mary Peckham,

82
00:03:29,089 --> 00:03:31,729
noticed how still the house was.

83
00:03:31,779 --> 00:03:33,969
She tried the doors.

84
00:03:34,019 --> 00:03:36,909
Joe's brother Ross came with a key.

85
00:03:36,959 --> 00:03:40,119
He opened the door to the room off the parlor.

86
00:03:40,169 --> 00:03:41,189
He told the coroner

87
00:03:41,239 --> 00:03:44,709
he did not wait long enough to see anything else.

88
00:03:44,759 --> 00:03:48,769
The town marshal, Hank Horton, went through every room.

89
00:03:48,819 --> 00:03:50,509
And then he did something

90
00:03:50,559 --> 00:03:52,039
almost nobody retells.

91
00:03:52,089 --> 00:03:54,179
According to the Moore store clerk,

92
00:03:54,229 --> 00:03:55,429
he locked the house

93
00:03:55,479 --> 00:03:58,019
and left the keys with the neighbor.

94
00:03:58,069 --> 00:03:59,379
Remember that.

95
00:03:59,429 --> 00:04:00,969
A locked house.

96
00:04:01,019 --> 00:04:03,809
A woman next door, holding the key.

97
00:04:03,859 --> 00:04:06,627
It is the last moment anyone could say

98
00:04:06,677 --> 00:04:08,109
who had been inside.

99
00:04:08,159 --> 00:04:09,669
When the doctors went in,

100
00:04:09,719 --> 00:04:11,989
every face was covered.

101
00:04:12,039 --> 00:04:14,002
Dr. Williams climbed the stairs

102
00:04:14,052 --> 00:04:15,949
to the room on the south side.

103
00:04:15,999 --> 00:04:18,709
A small boy, on his stomach.

104
00:04:18,759 --> 00:04:20,199
A gauze undershirt

105
00:04:20,250 --> 00:04:22,919
laid over the top of his head.

106
00:04:22,969 --> 00:04:26,479
Dr. Williams told the inquest what he did next.

107
00:04:26,529 --> 00:04:28,189
I lifted that off

108
00:04:28,239 --> 00:04:29,620
lifted it off

109
00:04:29,669 --> 00:04:31,059
to see which one it was.

110
00:04:36,731 --> 00:04:39,991
So here is the first inventory.

111
00:04:40,041 --> 00:04:44,361
An ax, near the bed where Lena and Ina were found.

112
00:04:44,411 --> 00:04:47,011
A piece of bacon, wrapped in a flour sack,

113
00:04:47,061 --> 00:04:48,971
at the head of that bed.

114
00:04:49,021 --> 00:04:52,861
A lamp on the floor, at the foot of the parents' bed.

115
00:04:52,911 --> 00:04:55,281
The marshal moved it out of the way.

116
00:04:55,331 --> 00:04:58,391
Skirts and aprons hung over the windows.

117
00:04:58,441 --> 00:05:00,321
Marks in the ceiling.

118
00:05:00,371 --> 00:05:03,041
Dr. Williams found no footprints.

119
00:05:03,091 --> 00:05:04,331
Five years later,

120
00:05:04,381 --> 00:05:05,493
the coroner testified

121
00:05:05,543 --> 00:05:08,331
there were no finger marks on the ax handle.

122
00:05:08,381 --> 00:05:10,941
Then the house was opened again.

123
00:05:10,991 --> 00:05:13,781
The National Guard came to hold the line.

124
00:05:13,831 --> 00:05:15,861
One account says ten thirty.

125
00:05:15,911 --> 00:05:17,941
Another says noon.

126
00:05:17,991 --> 00:05:21,541
How many people went in before that, I can't tell you.

127
00:05:21,591 --> 00:05:23,761
Later accounts say one hundred.

128
00:05:23,811 --> 00:05:25,831
Others say hundreds.

129
00:05:25,881 --> 00:05:28,571
I have not found a count from that day.

130
00:05:28,621 --> 00:05:29,921
What the record does show

131
00:05:29,971 --> 00:05:32,791
is a house entered again and again.

132
00:05:32,841 --> 00:05:35,511
Jessie Moore testified she went in that day,

133
00:05:35,561 --> 00:05:38,081
to get family pictures for the press.

134
00:05:38,131 --> 00:05:39,821
Bedding was burned.

135
00:05:39,871 --> 00:05:42,711
The house was cleaned, then repapered.

136
00:05:42,761 --> 00:05:43,701
So yes.

137
00:05:43,751 --> 00:05:45,501
Things were lost.

138
00:05:45,551 --> 00:05:48,731
But loss only explains what disappeared.

139
00:05:48,781 --> 00:05:50,551
It cannot explain what showed up.

140
00:05:51,133 --> 00:05:55,183
Item one arrives five days after the murders.

141
00:05:55,233 --> 00:05:58,783
A newspaper report, datelined Omaha.

142
00:05:58,833 --> 00:06:00,918
A woman named Retta Johnson

143
00:06:00,968 --> 00:06:03,133
has come back from Villisca.

144
00:06:03,183 --> 00:06:05,055
There is a closet, she says,

145
00:06:05,105 --> 00:06:08,213
off the room where the Stillinger girls slept.

146
00:06:08,263 --> 00:06:10,803
Bags of cotton batting inside.

147
00:06:10,853 --> 00:06:13,653
On them, the marks of a man

148
00:06:13,703 --> 00:06:15,143
who had sat and stood there.

149
00:06:15,193 --> 00:06:16,583
And in the girls' bed,

150
00:06:16,633 --> 00:06:17,233
a piece of watch chain.

151
00:06:16,633 --> 00:06:19,103
Now follow the closet.

152
00:06:19,153 --> 00:06:19,885
By the trial, five years on,

153
00:06:19,935 --> 00:06:21,143
the list of exhibits names a closet upstairs.

154
00:06:21,193 --> 00:06:26,693
A public comment posted under historian Edgar Epperly's name

155
00:06:26,743 --> 00:06:32,464
traces a version of the story to a Kansas City reporter,

156
00:06:32,514 --> 00:06:36,953
with cigarettes, and a heel print in the dust.

157
00:06:37,003 --> 00:06:39,233
It says an undertaker checked,

158
00:06:39,283 --> 00:06:42,513
and the box of cotton sat on two suit boxes

159
00:06:42,563 --> 00:06:43,873
that had not been crushed.

160
00:06:43,923 --> 00:06:46,783
Today, you will hear it was the attic.

161
00:06:46,833 --> 00:06:48,663
Now follow the chain.

162
00:06:48,713 --> 00:06:52,463
A watch chain, in a bed, in June.

163
00:06:52,513 --> 00:06:53,583
By the trial,

164
00:06:53,633 --> 00:06:55,163
a piece of key chain,

165
00:06:55,213 --> 00:06:58,343
and Joe Moore's watch, on the floor.

166
00:06:58,393 --> 00:07:00,473
The containers stayed the same.

167
00:07:00,523 --> 00:07:02,513
What was inside them kept changing.

168
00:07:03,166 --> 00:07:05,666
August, nineteen twelve.

169
00:07:05,716 --> 00:07:09,646
A man from Villisca tells people in Council Bluffs

170
00:07:09,696 --> 00:07:13,276
that detectives have a photograph of the killer.

171
00:07:13,326 --> 00:07:14,646
They got it, he says,

172
00:07:14,696 --> 00:07:18,836
from the retina of one of the Stillinger sisters.

173
00:07:18,886 --> 00:07:22,566
I have found no such photograph in any record.

174
00:07:22,616 --> 00:07:26,096
Her parents were alive to read that.

175
00:07:26,146 --> 00:07:27,886
By the end of that year,

176
00:07:27,936 --> 00:07:32,486
newspapers had reported at least five different men held or arrested.

177
00:07:32,536 --> 00:07:34,086
A brother-in-law.

178
00:07:34,136 --> 00:07:36,396
A man with stained shoes.

179
00:07:36,446 --> 00:07:39,666
A farmhand who wrote about a vision.

180
00:07:39,716 --> 00:07:41,276
A Sioux City porter,

181
00:07:41,326 --> 00:07:44,366
whom the headline identified by his race.

182
00:07:44,416 --> 00:07:46,056
A farmer.

183
00:07:46,106 --> 00:07:49,896
None of them was convicted of anything at Villisca.

184
00:07:49,946 --> 00:07:53,976
The question had stopped being what happened in the house.

185
00:07:54,026 --> 00:07:55,166
It had become

186
00:07:55,216 --> 00:07:56,406
who was going to pay for it.

187
00:07:57,083 --> 00:07:58,803
In nineteen sixteen,

188
00:07:58,853 --> 00:08:01,823
a private detective named James Wilkerson

189
00:08:01,873 --> 00:08:04,053
brought the case a theory.

190
00:08:04,103 --> 00:08:06,183
State senator Frank Jones,

191
00:08:06,233 --> 00:08:07,985
Joe Moore's old employer and business rival,

192
00:08:08,035 --> 00:08:09,053
had hired a killer.

193
00:08:09,103 --> 00:08:10,823
Wilkerson's man was William Mansfield.

194
00:08:10,873 --> 00:08:14,193
A grand jury refused to indict him.

195
00:08:14,243 --> 00:08:17,343
Frank Jones was never charged with anything.

196
00:08:17,393 --> 00:08:20,822
He sued Wilkerson for slander,

197
00:08:20,872 --> 00:08:24,293
asked for sixty thousand dollars,

198
00:08:24,343 --> 00:08:25,693
and lost.

199
00:08:25,743 --> 00:08:28,033
And in that lawsuit,

200
00:08:28,083 --> 00:08:30,493
a witness named Ed Landers took the oath.

201
00:08:30,543 --> 00:08:33,033
You have met Landers already,

202
00:08:33,083 --> 00:08:33,683
in a way.

203
00:08:33,083 --> 00:08:35,853
He was staying a few houses from the Moores.

204
00:08:35,903 --> 00:08:38,203
At the inquest in nineteen twelve,

205
00:08:38,253 --> 00:08:41,393
he described something he heard that Sunday night.

206
00:08:41,443 --> 00:08:42,803
A sound.

207
00:08:42,853 --> 00:08:45,883
Like one boy hooting for another.

208
00:08:45,933 --> 00:08:48,429
The next day, he said, it occurred to him

209
00:08:48,479 --> 00:08:50,603
it might have been a woman moaning.

210
00:08:50,653 --> 00:08:53,053
In the published summary of that testimony,

211
00:08:53,103 --> 00:08:54,903
that is all there is.

212
00:08:54,953 --> 00:08:55,993
Four years later,

213
00:08:56,043 --> 00:08:56,983
under oath,

214
00:08:57,033 --> 00:08:58,863
he said that on that same night

215
00:08:58,913 --> 00:09:01,453
he saw Frank Jones's son, Albert,

216
00:09:01,503 --> 00:09:03,683
go into the Moore home.

217
00:09:03,733 --> 00:09:06,263
Albert Jones was never charged either.

218
00:09:06,313 --> 00:09:09,023
Both men denied any part in it.

219
00:09:09,073 --> 00:09:12,593
The prosecution attacked Landers's account in open court.

220
00:09:12,643 --> 00:09:13,833
A sound

221
00:09:13,883 --> 00:09:14,573
became a man.

222
00:09:20,116 --> 00:09:24,696
In nineteen seventeen, the state found its own document.

223
00:09:24,746 --> 00:09:27,106
The Reverend Lyn George Kelly

224
00:09:27,156 --> 00:09:28,846
was a traveling preacher,

225
00:09:28,896 --> 00:09:31,296
in town for one Sunday.

226
00:09:31,346 --> 00:09:35,016
He was charged with the murder of Lena Stillinger.

227
00:09:35,066 --> 00:09:38,346
He had been charged with mailing obscene letters,

228
00:09:38,396 --> 00:09:41,296
and he had been held in a federal mental hospital.

229
00:09:41,346 --> 00:09:44,406
He caught the five nineteen train that morning.

230
00:09:44,456 --> 00:09:48,066
The train left hours before anyone opened the door.

231
00:09:48,116 --> 00:09:50,416
Witnesses swore he spoke of the murders

232
00:09:50,466 --> 00:09:52,706
before the bodies were found.

233
00:09:52,756 --> 00:09:55,176
They swore it five years later.

234
00:09:55,226 --> 00:09:59,246
A station ticket record backed up the date of one encounter.

235
00:09:59,296 --> 00:10:01,786
It could not record what was said.

236
00:10:01,836 --> 00:10:03,816
Then, Logan jail.

237
00:10:03,866 --> 00:10:05,556
August thirtieth.

238
00:10:05,606 --> 00:10:07,395
A newspaper editor testified

239
00:10:07,445 --> 00:10:10,376
that he was handcuffed and placed in Kelly's cell,

240
00:10:10,426 --> 00:10:12,516
playing an automobile thief.

241
00:10:12,566 --> 00:10:15,036
A sheriff read out accusations.

242
00:10:15,086 --> 00:10:18,396
The editor recalled eighty-seven paragraphs.

243
00:10:18,446 --> 00:10:19,576
Kelly said,

244
00:10:19,626 --> 00:10:20,226
that's a lie.

245
00:10:19,626 --> 00:10:25,046
Early the next morning, a county attorney took down a statement.

246
00:10:25,096 --> 00:10:27,456
Its first lines say it was made

247
00:10:27,506 --> 00:10:29,866
without any promise or threats.

248
00:10:29,916 --> 00:10:33,716
In it, a voice tells Kelly to slay utterly.

249
00:10:33,766 --> 00:10:37,506
He climbs the stairs, thinking they are Jacob's ladder.

250
00:10:37,556 --> 00:10:40,136
He kills the children first, it says.

251
00:10:40,186 --> 00:10:41,726
Then the parents.

252
00:10:41,776 --> 00:10:43,979
No record I have read can tell you

253
00:10:44,029 --> 00:10:46,496
whether that is the order it happened.

254
00:10:46,546 --> 00:10:48,510
The attorney general told the jury

255
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:50,524
Kelly had been asked fifteen times

256
00:10:50,574 --> 00:10:52,656
whether he killed the parents first.

257
00:10:52,706 --> 00:10:55,496
And fifteen times, he said no.

258
00:10:55,546 --> 00:10:59,546
Twenty-five times, he was told no promise could be made.

259
00:10:59,596 --> 00:11:03,306
Fifty-one times, he was told to tell the truth.

260
00:11:03,356 --> 00:11:05,006
The state counted those.

261
00:11:05,056 --> 00:11:08,066
It offered them as proof that he was free.

262
00:11:08,116 --> 00:11:09,866
At trial, Kelly said

263
00:11:09,916 --> 00:11:13,256
I have no recollection of signing that paper.

264
00:11:13,306 --> 00:11:15,486
The record shows a signature.

265
00:11:15,536 --> 00:11:18,136
It cannot show you what the signature is worth.

266
00:11:18,758 --> 00:11:22,508
There is one more name in the Omaha paper.

267
00:11:22,558 --> 00:11:26,408
A committee was raising money for Kelly's defense.

268
00:11:26,458 --> 00:11:27,898
It held meetings.

269
00:11:27,948 --> 00:11:28,548
It passed the hat.

270
00:11:27,948 --> 00:11:29,848
Its officers are listed.

271
00:11:29,898 --> 00:11:32,805
Remember the man who told the coroner

272
00:11:32,855 --> 00:11:34,688
my wife did, yes.

273
00:11:34,738 --> 00:11:37,005
She phoned about three different times.

274
00:11:37,055 --> 00:11:39,898
The state of Iowa had charged Kelly

275
00:11:39,948 --> 00:11:41,579
with killing his daughter.

276
00:11:41,629 --> 00:11:43,288
And Joseph Stillinger

277
00:11:43,338 --> 00:11:46,208
was the president of the committee

278
00:11:46,258 --> 00:11:47,478
trying to set him free.

279
00:11:52,981 --> 00:11:55,721
The paper does not say why.

280
00:11:55,771 --> 00:12:00,081
The committee said it wanted the guilty man brought to justice.

281
00:12:00,131 --> 00:12:02,641
It did not believe it had him.

282
00:12:02,691 --> 00:12:05,381
The first jury took twenty ballots.

283
00:12:05,431 --> 00:12:08,391
Eleven for acquittal, every time.

284
00:12:08,441 --> 00:12:14,031
One man, W. C. Brown, held for not guilty by reason of insanity.

285
00:12:14,081 --> 00:12:17,751
In November, a second jury acquitted Kelly.

286
00:12:17,801 --> 00:12:20,231
He was found not guilty.

287
00:12:20,281 --> 00:12:21,801
That is the verdict.

288
00:12:21,851 --> 00:12:24,171
There is one last item.

289
00:12:24,221 --> 00:12:26,431
March, nineteen thirty-one.

290
00:12:26,481 --> 00:12:28,272
A man in a Detroit jail,

291
00:12:28,322 --> 00:12:30,561
after five hours with detectives,

292
00:12:30,611 --> 00:12:33,411
confesses to the Villisca murders.

293
00:12:33,461 --> 00:12:34,562
The papers report

294
00:12:34,612 --> 00:12:38,351
that his fingerprints matched prints found at the scene.

295
00:12:38,401 --> 00:12:40,051
Remember the coroner.

296
00:12:40,101 --> 00:12:42,651
No finger marks on the handle.

297
00:12:42,701 --> 00:12:45,241
And this man's confession has a problem.

298
00:12:45,291 --> 00:12:47,141
He says he killed a man,

299
00:12:47,191 --> 00:12:48,291
his wife,

300
00:12:48,341 --> 00:12:50,141
and their four children.

301
00:12:50,191 --> 00:12:51,921
Six people.

302
00:12:51,971 --> 00:12:54,681
Officers said it did not tally.

303
00:12:54,731 --> 00:12:56,791
The last confession in the file

304
00:12:56,841 --> 00:12:59,121
forgot Lena and Ina.

305
00:13:04,615 --> 00:13:07,914
So here is the inventory now.

306
00:13:07,965 --> 00:13:09,235
A house.

307
00:13:09,284 --> 00:13:10,774
An ax.

308
00:13:10,825 --> 00:13:12,955
A few photographs.

309
00:13:13,004 --> 00:13:16,004
Everything else was carried in.

310
00:13:16,054 --> 00:13:19,514
Some of it, by people with something to gain.

311
00:13:19,565 --> 00:13:20,445
Much of it, I think,

312
00:13:20,495 --> 00:13:21,504
by people who could not stand an empty line on the form.

313
00:13:21,554 --> 00:13:26,225
That is what this kind of crime does.

314
00:13:26,274 --> 00:13:29,315
It leaves a space the exact size of a name.

315
00:13:29,365 --> 00:13:33,804
And a town will put anything in it.

316
00:13:33,855 --> 00:13:36,544
A stranger.

317
00:13:36,595 --> 00:13:37,894
A senator.

318
00:13:37,945 --> 00:13:39,264
A preacher who heard voices.

319
00:13:39,315 --> 00:13:42,075
No one has ever been convicted of these murders.

320
00:13:42,125 --> 00:13:45,725
Reverend Kelly was acquitted.

321
00:13:45,774 --> 00:13:51,174
Frank and Albert Jones were never charged.

322
00:13:51,225 --> 00:13:53,173
The one thing in this file

323
00:13:53,223 --> 00:13:56,085
that nobody needed to invent

324
00:13:56,134 --> 00:13:56,735
is a mother,

325
00:13:56,134 --> 00:13:57,455
on a Monday morning,

326
00:13:57,504 --> 00:13:59,014
calling a house.

327
00:13:59,065 --> 00:14:01,745
About three different times.

328
00:14:01,794 --> 00:14:06,514
This film is drawn from court testimony and newspapers of the time.

329
00:14:06,565 --> 00:14:08,764
A report is not proof.

330
00:14:08,815 --> 00:14:11,975
Everyone named here is presumed innocent of these murders.
